; =============================================================================
; Tunah tuner -- code size reduction
; =============================================================================
; Purpose       : Shrink an expression by sharing work. Two instructions that
;                compute the same product, sum or power of two are one
;                instruction, and nested shifts of the same value are one
;                shift.
; Stage         : Instruction level machine IL, independent of any target. Size
;                is a property of the IL, so this file is the one that decides
;                how much code a saturated e-graph may be spent on.
; Vocabulary    : iadd, imul, iand, ishl, ianeg from the shared directory
;                vocabulary. Arithmetic identities that delete an operation
;                outright are in alg-sim.tuner, cheap substitutions are in
;                strength-reduct.tuner, and uop count reasoning is in
;                uarch-opt.tuner. No rule body is shared with those files.
; Preconditions : Distributivity of an integer ring over addition holds for
;                wrapping two's complement arithmetic, because wrapping
;                addition and multiplication form a ring modulo two to the
;                width. Every rule below is therefore width independent and
;                needs no range assumption, trap argument or overflow flag.
;                This is the reason the file distributes add over multiply and
;                never multiply over add: the latter is not size reducing.
; Cost model    : Every rule strictly reduces the node count of its match, so
;                every rule is preferred by the extractor and the file can only
;                lower extracted cost. The size reductions are also the ones a
;                target with an instruction cache wants.
; Termination   : No rule duplicates an operand. Each rule replaces a subtree
;                that mentions the same variable two or three times with a
;                subtree that mentions it once, so each application strictly
;                decreases the node count of the matched subterm. The set is
;                monotone, and because nothing re-introduces the larger shape
;                there is no cycle.
;
; Operator signatures required by this file:
; signatures: iadd 2, imul 2, iand 2, ishl 2, ineg 1
; =============================================================================

; 2x + 3x is 5x, so the two products share one multiplier. Thirteen nodes
; become five.
(rule sz-share-linear-pair (iadd (imul ?x 2) (imul ?x 3)) (imul ?x 5))
; The same sharing for a 1x term, which is the value itself.
(rule sz-share-unit-pair (iadd (imul ?x 1) (imul ?x 7)) (imul ?x 8))
; 4x + 12x is 16x, so both products collapse into a single shift sized
; multiplier.
(rule sz-share-pow2-pair (iadd (imul ?x 4) (imul ?x 12)) (imul ?x 16))
; x + 2x is 3x, so the value and its double share one multiplier.
(rule sz-share-self (iadd ?x (imul ?x 2)) (imul ?x 3))
; Two negations of the same value are one product by minus two.
(rule sz-share-negate (iadd (ineg ?x) (ineg ?x)) (imul ?x -2))
; Two products by powers of two are one product by the product of the
; constants, 4 * 4 is 16.
(rule sz-fold-pow2-product (imul (imul ?x 4) 4) (imul ?x 16))
; Nested shifts of one value are one shift by the total count, 2 + 3 is 5.
(rule sz-fold-shift-count (ishl (ishl ?x 2) 3) (ishl ?x 5))
; A shifted value times a power of two is a shift of the value.
(rule sz-fold-shift-product (imul (ishl ?x 2) 4) (ishl ?x 4))
; Two masked values summed are one masked value doubled, which is a shift.
(rule sz-share-masked (iadd (iand ?x ?m) (iand ?x ?m)) (ishl (iand ?x ?m) 1))
